As things stand right now, if you summon a djinni through rubbing a
magic lamp or drinking a cloudy potion, and said djinni happens to be
hostile, he just beats the ever-loving crap out of you.  Where's the
fun in that?

I suggest that, if a cloudy potion or magic lamp is cursed, there
should be a chance that the angry djinni from it will pretend to act
like a wish-granting one, up until it actually becomes wish-granting
time.  Go ahead, wish for a blessed fixed wand of death, and the djinni
will make it appear - unfortunately, you failed to specify that said
wand should be in your hands, not those of that demon it just spawned a
few spaces away.  Want some gray dragon scale mail?  The djinni will be
more than happy to provide you with everything you need to make your
own - a blessed scroll of enchant armor and a colossal horde of gray
dragons.  Need a blessed scroll of genocide, and fast?  You'll get it.
You'll also get nine cursed scrolls of genocide.  (note: for obvious
reasons, the djinni will only play this particular trick if the player
is not a priest, does not have a blessed scroll of identify, and is not
on a level with an altar or store in it.).  Wishes for gold will spawn
precisely that number of gold golems (each holding 1 gold each when
killed).  Wishes for cockatrice corpses will create one on the ground
in front of you, then disintegrate every set of gloves in your
inventory.  Et cetera.

For whatever wishes couldn't be responded to with a clever trick, they
will be granted as if the player had wished through normal means, only
if he/she had incredibly bad luck.  Almost everything will be cursed
(unless cursed was specified, in which case the item will be uncursed),
all equipment will be -3 (unless, for some ungodly reason, -3 was
requested, in which case it'll be -2), etc.



